WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.200
Hi everyone, I'm Đorđe, welcome to the "Personal Finance" channel.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:14.500
While we wait for the end of the half-year to file the capital gains tax on trading Euro Commercial Properties shares, I hope you haven't forgotten about that,

3
00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:21.000
today I'd like to pass the time a bit and tell you about an interesting topic.

4
00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:27.500
I think it will interest you: it's about how to build your portfolio and how to structure it.

5
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.100
What are the results of this strategy?

6
00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.500
I'd show you in one picture.

7
00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:38.500
The green color shows my portfolio's performance, that is, the return in percent,

8
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:46.000
and the red dotted line shows the return of an ETF that tracks the S&P 500 index,

9
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.100
which can be considered a reference point, or benchmark.

10
00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:59.000
So let me show you how I built my portfolio and how I managed to get this result since the start of the year.

11
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.000
Before we continue, I have two big requests for you.

12
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:16.500
First: I noticed that many of you who watch my videos aren't subscribed to the channel at all, so I ask you all to subscribe and hit the bell.

13
00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:23.000
For you it's a couple of clicks, but for me it would really mean a lot.

14
00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:36.000
And the second big request: write to me in the comments whether you're interested in which program I use to track my portfolio, and whether you'd like to see a mini-lesson or tutorial on that topic.

15
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:45.200
Be sure to write me below; I think you'll find it interesting, but I wouldn't make something like that if not many people are interested.

16
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:53.000
And now, before we go further, it's important to stress: the figures I showed you are year-to-date.

17
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.000
They can change both up and down.

18
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:03.000
Past performance doesn't guarantee future returns, so I want to stress that once more.

19
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.300
And now let's define what a portfolio is at all.

20
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:20.670
I'd call a portfolio a collection that an investor builds: a collection of financial instruments or maybe some other items and other kinds of property, with the aim of earning income or preserving wealth.

21
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.500
That's said, so to speak, in my own words.

22
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:33.000
A portfolio can contain stocks, bonds, cryptocurrencies, gold, real estate — anything that comes to mind.

23
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:46.500
So when we build a portfolio, we need to take into account, for example, the investor's age, risk appetite, perhaps investment goals — whether they want long-term returns,

24
00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.400
whether they just want to preserve their assets, whether they want dividend income.

25
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:58.700
And perhaps most important: you need to pay attention to the correlation between the elements within the portfolio itself.

26
00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:07.000
For example, if the market falls, it's good to have instruments in the portfolio that behave well when the economy is in recession.

27
00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.500
For example, gold.

28
00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:16.500
I want to stress: there are actually very many theories about how a portfolio should be built.

29
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.000
And this will be just one of them.

30
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:29.200
I think most of us are busy with other work during the day, and we don't have time to sit in front of the computer, look at charts and actively rebalance the portfolio.

31
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.500
Of course, there are people who can do that, but most of us can't.

32
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:41.300
In the course of my research I arrived at one strategy, which can be briefly described as "core and satellites."

33
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.770
I use this strategy in my own portfolio too.

34
00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:50.510
If you follow this strategy, building a portfolio is very easy.

35
00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.430
First of all, you need to build the core.

36
00:03:53.430 --> 00:04:00.090
Into the core we'll put 70 to 80% of our investable funds.

37
00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:09.050
Since it's a large amount, I'd suggest here putting the money into something reliable, proven and without unnecessary risk.

38
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:23.810
There's one instrument that lets us do that — you'll guess: I'd suggest investing in an ETF, so that your core is one ETF or several.

39
00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:33.890
I'd suggest ETFs such as an ETF tracking the S&P 500 index, or an ETF containing stocks of more than 3000 companies from all over the world.

40
00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:49.290
That way most of your money earns the return that the market as a whole earns, and there's no extra risk tied to a single company, a single sector, and so on.

41
00:04:50.750 --> 00:05:00.070
And of course, once you decide which ETF to buy, all that's left is to decide whether you want an accumulating or a distributing ETF.

42
00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:05.270
I leave that decision to you, because each of us may have a different investment goal.

43
00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:14.290
So, after we've built the core, it's time to invest the remaining 20–30 percent of our funds

44
00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:20.330
into supporting, or so-called satellite, positions that revolve around our core.

45
00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:27.390
We'll build these positions according to our expectations of where returns will come from.

46
00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:34.530
For example, satellite positions can be used if we want to raise the dividend yield of our portfolio.

47
00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.850
Or if we believe some sector or region will be especially successful in the future

48
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:51.710
and we want to bet a little on that conviction and get a return higher than what the ETFs in our core will bring.

49
00:05:52.350 --> 00:06:00.770
For example, if you believe in technology and think technology is the future, you can invest in stocks of companies like NVIDIA and AMD,

50
00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:06.390
and thereby bet that these companies will outperform the ETF.

51
00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:15.070
Or, for example, you think China will advance a lot in the future, and you can buy an ETF that contains only Chinese companies.

52
00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:25.330
The decision is yours, of course, but these satellite positions can be anything. From stocks to ETFs — the options are simply limitless,

53
00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:35.130
and it's only a matter of deciding what you want to invest in and what, in your opinion, will outperform the ETF.

54
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:46.780
Of course, here too I'd stress: if you don't have an extra idea or the confidence to invest in something like this,

55
00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:57.120
you can always decide not to invest in satellite positions and keep all your money in the core, which is in fact the most reliable investment.

56
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:04.260
I'm not saying there's no risk, I'm only saying the risk is lower compared to investing in individual companies.

57
00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:16.260
If you don't have that confidence, it's absolutely fine to have only the core, meaning your whole portfolio is in one or several ETFs.

58
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.800
That's perfectly fine.

59
00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:31.000
On the other hand, if you decide to build a satellite position, I'd advise comparing the performance of your satellite positions with that of your core from time to time.

60
00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:39.360
If the satellite positions earned less than your core, I'm sorry to say this,

61
00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:47.600
but it's actually a sign that you're a bad investor and you pick stocks and these additional supporting positions badly.

62
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:58.860
I'm not saying you shouldn't do it, but maybe it's better to "park" your money in the core rather than in satellite positions.

63
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:16.740
Of course, the opposite case is possible too, where your satellite positions outperform the core, and that's perfectly fine — congratulations, you're a successful investor, but still you shouldn't neglect the core, because it's still the most reliable part of your portfolio.

64
00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:28.940
It's precisely because of all of this that I decided to close one of my satellite positions, precisely because I wasn't convinced it could outperform

65
00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:33.620
with its returns the returns that the ETF in my core gives.

66
00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:41.720
That's exactly why I decided to close the position and "park" my money in the safest place — in my core.

67
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:45.420
So, that's all about portfolios for today.

68
00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.360
I hope you liked this video.

69
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:55.360
I hope you learned something new and will try to apply this strategy in your investment portfolio.

70
00:08:56.040 --> 00:09:04.520
In the next video we'll be filing the capital gains tax, so if you don't want to miss that video, be sure to subscribe to the channel.

71
00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:05.700
Bye!